al since the enchantment level already triggers full reimbursement in that case. So I'll shift focus to improving the code structure itself. The validation loop that was recently added is currently inline within processClaim, and I think extracting it into a dedicated function with a clear name like `validateDamageCounts` would make the intent much more obvious and improve readability.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"All 30 tests pass. Now let me analyze the implementation, focusing on the recently added damage-count validation.\n\n## Refactoring Analysis\n\n### Step 1: Naming Evaluation (FIRST PRIORITY)\n\nThe most recent change added an inline validation loop inside `processClaim`:\n\n```\n- Current: validation logic is inline, unnamed, inside processClaim\n- Function purpose of processClaim: \"validate a claim then compute payout/cap\"\n- Question: Does the inline loop reveal its intent?\n- Assessment: The bare `for` loop with a throw is a distinct responsibility\n  (guarding against over-claiming) but has no name announcing that intent.\n- Recommendation: Extract to a well-named `assertDamagesWithinCoverage` helper\n  so processClaim reads as a sequence of named intentions.\n```\n\n### Step 2: Initial APP Mass (the validation block inside `processClaim`)\n\n```\nconst coveredCounts = countByType(policyItems);\nconst damageCounts = countByType(incident.damages.map((d) => ({ type: d.itemType })));\nfor (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n  if (count > (coveredCounts.get(type) ?? 0))\n    throw new Error(`More ${type} damages than insured`);\n}\n\n- Bindings: coveredCounts, damageCounts, type, count, damage/d = 5\n- Constants: template string ~1 = 1\n- Invocations: countByType x2, countByType(map), map, get, Error = 6 -> 12\n- Conditionals: if, ?? = 2 -> 8\n- Loops: for-of, map = 2 -> 10\n- Assignments: 0\nLocal block Total Mass: 5 + 1 + 12 + 8 + 10 = 36\n```\n\n### Step 3: Apply Simple Design Rules\n\n- **Rule 1 (Tests pass):*